From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 09:09:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 108/185] segacd: publish the CDD tick counter, live

Every timeout in the read path is counted in cdd_ticks, and cdd_ticks
only advances in the level 4 handler. If those 75 Hz status reports
never start then cd_time_left() is permanently true and a loop that
looks bounded is not -- which is the difference between a disk probe
that fails and one that hangs, and a Mode 1 boot hangs.

One word store in the handler, mirrored by IOFW into its report block.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 69d1785..ab8631f 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -773,6 +773,17 @@ void segacd_int_4_c(void)
 
     cdd_irqs++;
     cdd_ticks++;
+    /* Is this interrupt firing at all?
+     *
+     * Every bound in the read path is counted in cdd_ticks, and
+     * cdd_ticks only advances here. On a CD boot Sega's firmware has
+     * started the 75 Hz status reports long before EmuTOS sees the
+     * hardware; on a Mode 1 boot nothing has, and if they never start
+     * then cd_time_left() is permanently true and a loop that looks
+     * bounded is not. That is the difference between the disk probe
+     * failing and the disk probe hanging, and it is one word to tell
+     * them apart. IOFW mirrors this into its report block. */
+    *(volatile UWORD *)0xFF8026 = cdd_ticks;
 
     /* Wait for DRS to go *low*. It reads as "a status packet is on its
      * way in", so the BIOS spins until the incoming transfer is over
